Elias Israel:
 
> if the "mathematics by fiat"
> view (which seems quite reasonable to me) explains the preponderance of
> formal systems used for modeling, we're still left with the question of
> "why." What is it about the expressiveness of these systems that let's
> you bridge them over to areas where their rules don't apply?

Oh no! ... Forced beyond handy quotes from books on the shelf, I
must improvise!  I'd bet that this is well-plowed ground in some
dusty journal somewhere (and my scant exposure to the philosophy of
mathematics pertains largely to geometry), but I have a few thoughts:

* The rules ALWAYS apply when you ask a precise "objective" question.
  This is probably part of the same bias that underlies the math
  models: note that only formal questions can get formal answers.
  (Douglas Adams exploits a censor on this topic by responding to "what
  is the answer to the ultimate questions of life?" with "42").
  I sometimes wonder if we don't sometimes ask formal questions in 
  inappropriate contexts (thus is born sociology).

* Anyway, given that we're asking formal questions or expecting formal
  statements, what we're really doing is looking for functions that
  map inputs onto outputs in a way "similar" to observation.  What
  math is all about is developing concise notations for expressing
  some of these mappings (the "useful" ones).  The question then
  becomes why a relatively small number of notational devices
  for functions, under limited composition, match observations.

* I begin by noting that the universe of "interesting" phenomena is
  not random -- which helps a great deal!  Departures from randomness
  can *drastically* reduce the description length; especially when we're
  really talking about approximate descriptions, which is the contention
  of the "math by fiat" view.  Some of the reasons are probably "basic" --
  causality (logic, most everything else), continuity (geometry),
  dimensionality (geometry), "agglomeration" (number theory),
  force fields (algebra, analysis), ...

* This links intimately with our biology:  if there weren't exploitable
  regularities in our environment, we couldn't exist because evolutionary
  processes couldn't operate.  Similarly, our abilities to detect such
  regularities are highly tied to our internal "interestingness" detectors.

* The implication (if this limb isn't creaking too loudly yet) is that in
  a sense, we're biased to think that phenomena with certain regularity
  properties, specific to our biology, are interesting (catch our attention
  and prod us to ask questions about them).

* It is exactly these regularities that allow concise notations to serve
  as (approximate, at least) models for the phenomena we find interesting.

Call this the "DZ" (pronounced "dizzy") theory of mathematical efficacy.
I don't think it applies to fundamental physical laws; those are probably
embedded in the computational device responsible for the "simulation"
that is our universe.

One interesting result is that there should be a very large number
of useful notational devices (some that correspond to our biological
interestingness detectors and many that do not) yet undiscovered to
serve as useful approximate models of aspects of reality.

> it seems to me that the "mathematics by
> fiat" view argues that formal systems aren't the best tool for at least
> some kinds of mental exploration.

