Date: Tue, 20 Apr 93 00:55:45 -0700
From: davisd@pierce.ee.washington.edu
Subject: EPIST: Why do formal systems work? 

> From: dstalder@gmuvax2.gmu.edu (Darren/Torin/Who ever...)
> 
> I didn't want to get into this, but I can't resist.  Many systems are
> preferred due to simplicity.  William of Ockam said it: "It is vain to do
> with more what can be done with less...Entities should not be multiplied
> beyond necessity."  In other words, if two systems both work and one is
> simpler, the simpler one is probably the one with better predictive power.
> (Closer to true-to-fact.) 

What Ockham does, for the most part, is convert entities into
attributes whenever possible. For example, he would not say that you
have a thought, he would say that you think. I personally find that 
a very effective strategy, but I wouldn't rule out using thoughts as
entities on occasions, or in general, rule out models with more
entities. 

Whatever system provides the best predictive power is the one to use,
regardless of the number of entities involved. I was interested for
a while in a few researchers who were attempting to invoke either
Occam's Rule, or variants thereof, when modelling data. At some point,
they are always required to give some criteria for simplicity. By what
standard is one thing simpler than another? And they do come up with
what seem to most like reasonable simplicity standards, but at bottom,
these standards are perfectly arbitrary, and indeed have been shown in
cases to lead to absurd conclusions. 

Usually, if they start with models which are identical in form and
construction except for the number of a certain kind of parameter, and
two models give the same performance on the training data, the one
with fewer parameters does give better performance on off training set
data. Our generalized rule proves weaker and weaker as the models
differ more and more in training, construction, and form.

The best model depends in the end on who is doing the modeling, what
other models they have created, and how well the candidate models mesh
with our subjects original model. And of course, the proof of the
pudding is in the final performance of our modeller.

