A long time ago, I wrote, and Craig Presson answered: 

|> confused and open-minded about the private property thing until I met
|> Anton Sherwood, who handed me a copy of Friedman's tMoF, in which for
|> the first time I found a coherent system of political ideas that I was
|> entirely comfortable with.
 
 CP> Careful, now. Ddfr is not "entirely comfortable" with or within
 CP> tMoF, so I don't think you get to be either. Of course, he's
 CP> around here on the list somewhere and can decide for himself how
 CP> much hero-worship he wants :-)
 
Hmm... that's not quite what I meant. Friedman's "discomfort" was
exactly what was making me comfortable!
 
All, and I do mean all, of the libertarian writings I had encountered
before Ddfr were entirely lacking in such "discomfort". Ayn Rand, of
course, is Exhibit A. But even Leonard Read, Frederic Bastiat, Henry
Hazlitt, and most of the decent propagandists for the LP seem entirely
too *certain* about matters of black and white, er, I mean, right and
wrong. I think one reason I remained sympathetic to the leftist
anarchists (for instance, Emma Goldman) as long as I did was that such
annoying (sanctimonious, even) certitude is less conspicuous in their
writings than in that of many of the twentieth-century classical
liberals.
 
Of course, the sort of foolish 1930's fascist moral relativism that
Rand was fighting in her novels made the absolutist rhetoric she used
much more effective then than it is now. Fortunately we aren't living
in the early twentieth century anymore. (And lest anyone think I hate
Rand, while I think her epistemology is nonsense, I'm in the middle of
my third time through Atlas Shrugged, just for pleasure this time.)
 
David Friedman was the first political thinker I encountered who was
both comfortably skeptical about his political and moral ideas and yet
fully rational at the same time. And his essay "In Defense of
Property" was the piece that finally brought me to an understanding of
what the institution of private property really is (I think). :-) "In
Defense of Property" is what blew away the last bits of intellectual
sympathy I harbored for the leftist anarchists. (Of course, I still
harbor emotional sympathy for anarchists of any stripe whatsoever...
but with the recognition that the ones who want to do away with
property institutions are living dangerously in a fantasy world.)
 
(BTW, for those who haven't read it, "In Defense of Property" is one
of the first chapters in Ddfr's book The Machinery of Freedom.)
 
