Subject: wiretap chip

After reading all the recent posts about the wiretap chip, I think one
of the best things we can do is tell derogatory jokes about it and
AT&T. If it becomes the butt of jokes widespread in the computers and
communications profession before AT&T releases the first models of
Wiretap Chip phones, then RSA might have a decent chance to get a
second commercial standard into place. Jokes are good self-propagating
memes.
 
The American business community doesn't always follow the government's
lead in these things. We've still never gone metric, for instance. OSI
still has not budged the IP suite of protocols (or whatever it's
called this week). If people don't trust the Wiretap Chip, there will
be a large enough market to support a business standard side-by-side
with the government standard.
 
If a second commercial standard for voice encryption is in place on
the market in the next two years or so, then the government will have
a much harder time outlawing it. I only hope that RSA (or someone) has
the capital to force the issue that soon.
 
And I completely agree with Tom Jennings that "the wiretap chip" is a
much better tag to hang on this affair than "Big Brother", my previous
comments about SDI and Star Wars to the side.
 
